## Question D: ALL Liabilities Introduced in This Bill (Civil and Criminal), With Conditions

### I. Criminal Liabilities (Offences)

All offences below are strict regulatory offences triable either summarily or on indictment unless noted. "Continuing offence" daily fines apply where stated.

| # | Offence | Clause | Who Liable | Conditions Giving Rise to Liability | Penalty |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | **Failure to comply with a regulator's direction** | 7(8)–(9) | CI operator | Fails to comply with a written direction under s. 7(1) or (2) (given where the operator has failed to comply with, or its compliance with, a Category 1, 2 or 3 obligation is defective/failed). | Summary: fine **$3,000,000** + $60,000/day continuing. Indictment: fine **$5,000,000** + $100,000/day continuing. |
| 2 | **Failure to provide information** required for designation/compliance purposes | 18(1)–(2) | Any organization; **higher penalties if a CI operator at the time of the offence** | Without reasonable excuse, fails to comply with s. 14(2), 15(3), 16(2) or 17(3) (information notices). | *If CI operator:* Summary: **$3,000,000** + $60,000/day; Indictment: **$5,000,000** + $100,000/day. *Otherwise:* Summary: **$300,000** + $30,000/day; Indictment: **$500,000** + $50,000/day. |
| 3 | **Failure to maintain a Hong Kong office / notify correspondence address or address change** | 19(4)–(5) | CI operator | Fails to comply with s. 19(1) (maintain office; notify address within 1 month of designation or extended period) or s. 19(3) (notify change of address within 1 month). | Summary: **$300,000** + $30,000/day continuing; Indictment: **$500,000** + $50,000/day continuing. |
| 4 | **Failure to notify an operator change** | 20(2)–(3) | CI operator | Fails to notify the regulator in writing of a change of the organization operating the infrastructure, as soon as practicable and within 1 month. | Summary: **$3,000,000** + $60,000/day continuing; Indictment: **$5,000,000** + $100,000/day continuing. |
| 5 | **Failure to notify appointment/change of supervisor of the security management unit** | 21(7)–(8) | CI operator | Fails to comply with s. 21(4)(b) (notify appointment of adequately knowledgeable employee to supervise the unit within the specified period) or s. 21(6) (notify any change to the appointment within 1 month). | Summary: **$300,000** + $30,000/day continuing; Indictment: **$500,000** + $50,000/day continuing. |
| 6 | **Failure to notify material changes to critical computer systems** | 22(4)–(5) | CI operator | Fails to notify the regulator within 1 month of any s. 22(2) event (material change to design/configuration/security/operation of a critical computer system; removal of a critical computer system; addition of a system essential to the core function; change making an existing system essential). | Summary: **$300,000** + $30,000/day continuing; Indictment: **$500,000** + $50,000/day continuing. |
| 7 | **Failure to submit (or submit revisions of) a computer-system security management plan** | 23(7)–(8) | CI operator | Fails to submit the plan within 3 months of designation (or extended period) or submit a revision within 1 month of the revision being made. *(Note: failure to "implement" the plan under s. 23(5) is not itself an offence under s. 23(7), but may trigger directions under s. 7 or directed audits under s. 25(4).)* | Summary: **$300,000** + $30,000/day continuing; Indictment: **$500,000** + $50,000/day continuing. |
| 8 | **Failure to conduct risk assessments / submit assessment reports / comply with directed assessments** | 24(8)–(9) | CI operator | Fails to comply with s. 24(1) (conduct assessments within 12 months of designation and every 12 months thereafter; submit reports within 3 months of the assessment deadline or extended period) or a requirement imposed under s. 24(5) (regulator-directed assessment after a s. 22 event). | Summary: **$300,000** + $30,000/day continuing; Indictment: **$500,000** + $50,000/day continuing. |
| 9 | **Failure to arrange audits / submit audit reports / comply with directed audits** | 25(10)–(11) | CI operator | Fails to comply with s. 25(1) (arrange independent audits within 24 months of designation and every 24 months thereafter; submit reports within 3 months of the audit deadline or extended period) or a requirement under s. 25(4) or (6) (regulator-directed audits). | Summary: **$300,000** + $30,000/day continuing; Indictment: **$500,000** + $50,000/day continuing. |
| 10 | **Failure to participate in a computer-system security drill** | 26(3)–(4) | CI operator | Fails to comply with a Commissioner's requirement under s. 26(2) to participate in a drill (after reasonable written notice). | Summary: fine **$3,000,000**; Indictment: fine **$5,000,000**. *(No continuing-offence fine.)* |
| 11 | **Failure to submit (or submit revisions of) an emergency response plan** | 27(7)–(8) | CI operator | Fails to submit the plan within 3 months of designation (or extended period) or submit a revision within 1 month. *(As with s. 23, failure to "implement" under s. 27(5) is not itself an offence under s. 27(7).)* | Summary: **$300,000** + $30,000/day continuing; Indictment: **$500,000** + $50,000/day continuing. |
| 12 | **Failure to notify a computer-system security incident / submit written record / submit written report** | 28(5)–(6) | CI operator | Fails to comply with s. 28(1) (notify the Commissioner as soon as practicable and within the specified time — **12 hours** if the incident disrupts/is disrupting/is likely to disrupt the core function, otherwise **48 hours**), s. 28(3) (written record within 48 hours if initial notification not in specified form), or s. 28(4) (written report within **14 days** of becoming aware). | Summary: fine **$3,000,000**; Indictment: fine **$5,000,000**. *(No continuing-offence fine.)* |
| 13 | **Failure to comply with a specified requirement in early intervention or computer-system security investigations** | 42(1)–(3) | Organization | Without reasonable excuse, fails to comply with a specified requirement — i.e., a requirement imposed under Part 5 Division 1 (early intervention, ss. 29–32) or Division 2 (investigations, ss. 33–40), or under a s. 37 or s. 38 warrant. Self-incrimination is expressly **not** a reasonable excuse (s. 42(2)). | Summary: fine **$300,000**; Indictment: fine **$500,000**. |
| 14 | **Failure to comply with a requirement in an offence investigation** | 45(1)–(3) | Organization | Without reasonable excuse, fails to comply with a requirement imposed under s. 43 (offence investigation). Self-incrimination is expressly **not** a reasonable excuse (s. 45(2)). | Summary: fine **$300,000**; Indictment: fine **$500,000**. |
| 15 | **Breach of secrecy by specified persons** | 58(1), (3); 57(1) | Specified persons (current/former regulating authorities, authorized officers, delegates, appeal panel/board members, members of regulating-authority bodies, related persons and their staff) | Contravenes s. 57(1): outside performance of functions under the Ordinance, suffers/permits access to, or communicates, matter relating to a person's affairs obtained in connection with performing functions under the Ordinance, other than to the person to whom it relates. | Summary: fine at **level 6** + imprisonment **6 months**; Indictment: fine **$1,000,000** + imprisonment **2 years**. |
| 16 | **Unauthorized re-disclosure by recipients of disclosed information** | 58(2)–(3); 57(5) | Any person who receives information disclosed under s. 57(1), (2) or (3) (other than s. 57(2)(a) or (3)(c)), or who obtains it from such a recipient | Discloses the information to any other person in contravention of s. 57(5), where at the time: (i) the person knew or ought to have known the information was previously disclosed under s. 57(1), (2) or (3) (excluding s. 57(2)(a) or (3)(c)); and (ii) the person had no reasonable grounds to believe s. 57(5) did not apply by virtue of s. 57(6) (e.g., regulator consent, public availability, legal-advice purposes, proceedings, court/tribunal order or legal requirement). | Summary: fine at **level 6** + imprisonment **6 months**; Indictment: fine **$1,000,000** + imprisonment **2 years**. |
| 17 | **Offences under regulations made by the Secretary for Security** | 69(2)–(4) | Persons prescribed by future regulations | Contravention of regulations made for the better carrying out of the Ordinance. | Summary: fine up to **$3,000,000** (+ up to $60,000/day continuing); Indictment: fine up to **$5,000,000** (+ up to $100,000/day continuing). |
| 18 | **Conspiracy to commit specified offences** | 56(1)(e) | Any person | Conspiring to commit any of the offences prosecutable by designated authorities under s. 56(1)(a)–(d) (s. 7 direction breaches, s. 18 information breaches, Category 1/2 breaches by their operators, s. 45 investigation breaches). | Triable summarily before a magistrate (per s. 56(2)); penalties as per the underlying offence. |

**Important structural notes on criminal liability:**
- **Defences available:** (i) **Due diligence** (clause 65) — for offences under s. 7 or Part 4, the defendant is acquitted if the offence was due to a cause beyond their control and they took all reasonable precautions and exercised all due diligence (with notice requirements and cooperation conditions where blaming a third party's act/omission or information); (ii) **Reasonable excuse** (clause 66) — where an offence provision references reasonable excuse, it operates as a defence, established if sufficient evidence is adduced and not disproved beyond reasonable doubt.
- **Self-incrimination:** Compelled explanations/answers that might incriminate are inadmissible in criminal proceedings **except** proceedings for offences under s. 42 or s. 45, or under Part V of the Crimes Ordinance (Cap. 200) (clauses 41 and 44) — meaning the compelled material *can* be used in those specified proceedings.
- **Codes of practice:** Failure to observe a code of practice does **not by itself** create civil or criminal liability (clause 9(1)), but codes are admissible and non-observance may be relied on as evidence in proceedings (clause 9(2)); regulators may also treat non-observance as relevant to whether compliance is "defective" for direction purposes (clause 7(6)).

### II. Civil Liabilities

The bill is notable for what it **does not** create, and for the immunities it grants:

1. **No new civil cause of action against CI operators is created by the bill itself.** The bill's enforcement scheme is entirely criminal/administrative (fines, directions, investigations). There is no provision making a CI operator civilly liable to third parties for security incidents, and no express private right of action.

2. **No civil liability from non-observance of codes of practice** — clause 9(1): "A failure by an organization to observe a provision of a code of practice does not by itself make the organization liable to any civil or criminal proceedings." (However, code compliance evidence remains admissible in any civil proceedings that exist independently — clause 9(2).)

3. **Civil immunity granted (which conversely defines the civil liability landscape):**
   - **Clause 60(1):** A person who **complies with a direction or requirement** imposed by or under the Ordinance does **not** incur any civil liability (in contract, tort, defamation, equity or otherwise) *by reason only of that compliance*. This protects, e.g., a third-party organization compelled under a s. 37 warrant to take a customer's system offline — it cannot be sued for doing so.
   - **Clause 60(2):** A person does **not** incur civil liability for an act or omission done **in good faith** in the performance or purported performance of any function under the Ordinance. **Clause 60(3):** this does not affect the liability of the **Government** for the act or omission — i.e., the Government itself remains potentially liable.

4. **Residual civil liability preserved:** The immunities are narrow. A CI operator (or third party) remains subject to any ordinary civil liability (e.g., negligence, contract) arising from a security incident **except** where clause 60(1) or (2) applies. The bill neither expands nor abolishes general civil law.

### III. Administrative/Quasi-Liability Consequences (Not Fines, But Adverse Legal Consequences)

While not "liabilities" in the strict sense, the following adverse consequences arise from the bill and are worth noting:

- **Directions compelling action or inaction** (clause 7) — non-compliance converts these into the criminal offence at #1 above.
- **Revocation of designations** (clauses 12(4), 13(2)) and **revocation of exemptions** (clause 55(5)) — loss of status or exemption where conditions are contravened or the public interest changes.
- **Compelled operational measures** — being required to cease using a system, preserve its state, scan it, or carry out remedial measures (clauses 36(2), 37(2), 38(2)) — with criminal exposure for non-compliance (#13 above).
- **Search and seizure** — entry by force, seizure of documents and access to systems under warrants (clauses 31, 37, 38, 46) or in emergencies without warrant (clause 40).
- **Appeal outcomes** — an appeal board may confirm, vary or reverse the contested decision (clause 49(1)); lodging an appeal does not stay the decision unless the board orders a stay (clause 48(3)–(6)).
- **Property disposal** — property seized is dealt with under s. 102 of the Criminal Procedure Ordinance (Cap. 221) as if held by police in connection with an offence (clause 64).

### Summary Table of Maximum Penalties

| Offence Type | Summary Conviction | Conviction on Indictment |
|---|---|---|
| Direction non-compliance (s. 7) | $3,000,000 (+$60,000/day) | $5,000,000 (+$100,000/day) |
| Information non-compliance — CI operator (s. 18) | $3,000,000 (+$60,000/day) | $5,000,000 (+$100,000/day) |
| Information non-compliance — other organizations (s. 18) | $300,000 (+$30,000/day) | $500,000 (+$50,000/day) |
| Operator change notification (s. 20) | $3,000,000 (+$60,000/day) | $5,000,000 (+$100,000/day) |
| Office/address (s. 19); unit supervisor notification (s. 21); system change notification (s. 22); management plan (s. 23); risk assessments (s. 24); audits (s. 25); emergency response plan (s. 27) | $300,000 (+$30,000/day) | $500,000 (+$50,000/day) |
| Drill participation (s. 26) | $3,000,000 | $5,000,000 |
| Incident notification (s. 28) | $3,000,000 | $5,000,000 |
| Investigation requirement non-compliance (ss. 42, 45) | $300,000 | $500,000 |
| Secrecy breaches (s. 58) | Level 6 fine + 6 months' imprisonment | $1,000,000 + 2 years' imprisonment |
| Future regulations (s. 69) | Up to $3,000,000 (+$60,000/day) | Up to $5,000,000 (+$100,000/day) | [end of text]


